## Why This Problem Is Asked
This problem tests your ability to design multi-dimensional DP by combining deletion constraints with pairwise scoring across adjacent characters. It evaluates state space design, transition logic, and reconstruction from a full solution path.

## Key Concepts
- **State design**: A 3D DP tracks position i (0 to n), deletions used d (0 to k), and the last kept character L (0–25, or 26 for none).
- **Transition logic**: The delete transition preserves state; the keep transition accumulates score only when a previous character exists, using W[prev][cur].
- **Reconstruction**: Backtracking from the optimal endpoint to determine which characters were deleted.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxScore(s, W, k) {
    const n = s.length, NONE = 26;
    const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () =>
        Array.from({length: k+1}, () =>
            Array(NONE+1).fill(-Infinity)));
    dp[0][0][NONE] = 0;

    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++) {
            for (let L = 0; L <= NONE; L++) {
                if (dp[i][d][L] === -Infinity) continue;
                // Delete
                if (d < k) dp[i+1][d+1][L] = Math.max(dp[i+1][d+1][L], dp[i][d][L]);
                // Keep
                const cur = s.charCodeAt(i) - 97;
                const gain = L === NONE ? 0 : W[L][cur];
                dp[i+1][d][cur] = Math.max(dp[i+1][d][cur], dp[i][d][L] + gain);
            }
        }
    }
    // Find best among d ≤ k
    let best = 0, bestD = 0, bestL = NONE;
    for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++)
        for (let L = 0; L <= NONE; L++)
            if (dp[n][d][L] > best) { best = dp[n][d][L]; bestD = d; bestL = L; }
    return { score: best, deletions: bestD, lastKept: bestL };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- **Memory optimization**: Can you reduce space to O(27·k) using rolling arrays?
- **Negative weights**: How would you handle cases where W contains negative values—would your approach change?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem extends classic knapsack-style dynamic programming to incorporate time-based constraints (deadlines) and a limit on the number of selectable jobs. It tests understanding of multi-dimensional DP states, proper handling of time as a resource, and the ability to reconstruct the solution.

## Key Concepts
- Scheduling with deadlines and profits
- 3D DP formulation tracking (jobs processed, jobs selected, time)
- Backward iteration to avoid reusing the same job
- Reconstruction via predecessor pointers
- Time complexity: O(n × K × D), where D is the maximum deadline

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxProfitJobs(jobs, K) {
  jobs.sort((a, b) => a.d - b.d);
  const maxT = Math.max(...jobs.map(j => j.d));
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: K + 1 }, () => Array(maxT + 1).fill(NEG));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: K + 1 }, () => Array(maxT + 1).fill(null));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (const { p, d, w } of jobs) {
    for (let k = K - 1; k >= 0; k--) {
      for (let t = d; t >= p; t--) {
        if (dp[k][t - p] !== NEG) {
          const newProfit = dp[k][t - p] + w;
          if (newProfit > dp[k + 1][t]) {
            dp[k + 1][t] = newProfit;
            prev[k + 1][t] = { prevK: k, prevT: t - p };
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  let bestProfit = 0, bestK = 0, bestT = 0;
  for (let k = 1; k <= K; k++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= maxT; t++) {
      if (dp[k][t] > bestProfit) {
        bestProfit = dp[k][t];
        bestK = k;
        bestT = t;
      }
    }
  }

  const selected = [];
  let [k, t] = [bestK, bestT];
  while (k > 0) {
    const { prevK, prevT } = prev[k][t];
    selected.push({ finishTime: t });
    k = prevK;
    t = prevT;
  }

  return { profit: bestProfit, jobs: selected.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests fixed-length path DP and path reconstruction in graphs, a core DP pattern that shows depth beyond standard knapsack.

## Key Concepts
- DP on path length t
- State transition using edges
- Backpointer reconstruction
- Time and space trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKEdgePath(n, edges, k) {
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let v=0; v<n; v++) dp[v][0] = 0;
  for (let t=1; t<=k; t++) {
    for (const e of edges) {
      const {u,v,w} = e;
      const prev = dp[u][t-1];
      if (prev !== NEG) {
        const cand = prev + w;
        if (cand > dp[v][t]) {
          dp[v][t] = cand;
          par[v][t] = u;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let bestV = 0, best = NEG;
  for (let v=0; v<n; v++) if (dp[v][k] > best) { best = dp[v][k]; bestV = v; }
  const path = [];
  let cur = bestV;
  for (let t=k; t>=1; t--) { path.push(cur); cur = par[cur][t]; }
  path.push(cur);
  path.reverse();
  return {path, weight: best};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would this change if edges could have negative weights? How to handle ties in reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked
Offers a new DP angle: path with exact turns, combining grid path DP with turn accounting. Tests deriving a tight state space, base cases, and reconstruction, mirroring routing-like constraints in real systems.

## Key Concepts
- Grid DP with an extra dimension for turns
- Two direction states to capture turns
- Reconstruction via backpointers
- Careful base/edge-case handling (start cell, negative infinities)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithTurns(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  // 4D DP: [i][j][t][d]
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: m}, () =>
    Array.from({length: K+1}, () => [NEG, NEG])));
  dp[0][0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  dp[0][0][0][1] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        if (i === 0 && j === 0) continue;
        // last move right
        if (j > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][0] = Math.max(
            (dp[i][j-1][t][0] !== NEG ? dp[i][j-1][t][0] : NEG),
            (t > 0 && dp[i-1][j][t-1][1] !== NEG ? dp[i-1][j][t-1][1] : NEG)
          ) + grid[i][j];
        }
        // last move down
        if (i > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][1] = Math.max(
            (dp[i-1][j][t][1] !== NEG ? dp[i-1][j][t][1] : NEG),
            (t > 0 && dp[i][j-1][t-1][0] !== NEG ? dp[i][j-1][t-1][0] : NEG)
          ) + grid[i][j];
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return Math.max(dp[n-1][m-1][K][0], dp[n-1][m-1][K][1]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to allow at most k turns and also maximize path length if grid values included negative numbers?
- How would you optimize space to O(nmK) memory or better, and what would be the trade-offs for reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked
This interview question tests dynamic programming with prefix-state tracking and solution reconstruction. The inner minimum calculation forces careful leftward scanning, providing a tangible beginner-to-intermediate challenge while remaining implementable in O(n²k) time.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming over prefixes with block count tracking
- On-the-fly maintenance of minimum values over sliding windows
- Solution reconstruction via backpointers
- Time complexity: O(n²k), Space complexity: O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueBlocks(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const pre = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(k, i); t++) {
      let currentMin = Infinity;
      for (let p = i; p >= t; p--) {
        currentMin = Math.min(currentMin, A[p-1]);
        const candidate = (dp[p-1][t-1] + currentMin * (i-p+1));
        if (candidate > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = candidate;
          pre[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct solution
  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const p = pre[i][t];
    blocks.unshift([p, i]);
    i = p - 1;
    t--;
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a non-trivial DP over a linear sequence with a non-crossing constraint and reconstruction of the chosen pairs. It balances readability with the need to optimize across many left endpoints efficiently.

## Key Concepts

- Non-crossing pairings on a line
- DP by right endpoint with reconstruction
- Time/space trade-offs; potential 1D optimization for i dimension

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxNonCrossingPairs(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[i][0] = 0;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      let best = i>0 ? dp[i-1][t] : 0;
      let bestJ = -1;
      for(let j=0;j<i;j++){
        const left = (j>=1) ? dp[j-1][t-1] : (t-1===0 ? 0 : NEG);
        const val = left + Math.abs(A[i]-A[j]);
        if(val > best){ best = val; bestJ = j; }
      }
      dp[i][t] = best;
      take[i][t] = bestJ;
    }
  }
  // reconstruction sketch (omitted for brevity)
  return { value: dp[n-1][k], pairs: [] };
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you enforce exactly k pairs instead of at most k?
- Can you optimize memory to O(n) or derive a monotone queue optimization for certain A distributions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests concrete DP skills on string partitioning with reconstruction; approachable for beginners yet non-trivial.

## Key Concepts
- dp[i][t]: maximum sum for first i digits split into t parts
- val(j,i): integer value of s[j..i-1] (leading zeros allowed)
- transition: dp[i][t] = max_{j ∈ [t-1..i-1]} dp[j][t-1] + val(j,i)
- reconstruction: track prev to recover split indices
- complexity: O(n²k) time, O(nk) space; use BigInt for large results

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumSplit(s, k){
  const n = s.length;
  const val = (a,b)=> BigInt(s.slice(a,b));
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1n));
  const prev = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    dp[i][1] = val(0,i);
  }

  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let j=t-1;j<i;j++){
        const candidate = dp[j][t-1] + val(j,i);
        if(candidate > dp[i][t]){
          dp[i][t] = candidate;
          prev[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct splits
  const splits = [];
  let curr = n, parts = k;
  while(parts > 1){
    const j = prev[curr][parts];
    splits.push(j);
    curr = j;
    parts--;
  }
  splits.reverse();

  return {maxSum: dp[n][k], splits};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
New angle: block value defined as distinct counts, forcing explicit precomputation and a clear reconstruction path. Tests DP over prefixes with segment costs that depend on subarray content, not just sums.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix DP with exact block count
- Precompute interval distinct counts C[l][r]
- Reconstruct endpoints via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
// DP sketch for distinct-count blocks
let n = A.length;
let C = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(n).fill(0));
for (let L=0; L<n; L++) {
  let seen = new Map();
  let d = 0;
  for (let R=L; R<n; R++) {
    if (!seen.has(A[R])) { seen.set(A[R],1); d++; }
    C[L][R] = d;
  }
}
let dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
let par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
dp[0][0] = 0;
for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
  for (let t=1; t<=k; t++) {
    for (let p=t-1; p<=i-1; p++) {
      let val = dp[p][t-1] + C[p][i-1];
      if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to model partitioning with a non-linear block value, a common DP pattern in beginner interviews. Requires prefix sums for O(1) block sums, a clear state definition over prefix length and blocks, and backtracking to output endpoints.

## Key Concepts
- DP on partitions with exact k blocks
- Prefix sums for subarray sums
- State: dp[i][t], parent pointers for reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxRevenue(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1] = S[i]*i;
  for (let t=2;t<=k;t++) {
    for (let i=t;i<=n;i++) {
      for (let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t-1] + (S[i]-S[p])*(i-p);
        if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction
  let i=n, t=k; const ends=[];
  while (t>0) { const p = par[i][t]; ends.push([p+1, i]); i = p; t--; }
  ends.reverse();
  return {value: dp[n][k], windows: ends};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) time using a trick for monotone optima?
- How would you adapt if windows could overlap or if k is variable?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP design when block value is nonlinear in length and sum, forcing nontrivial transitions and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping subarrays DP, prefix sums, reconstruction pointers
- Time/space trade-offs with O(n^2 k) baseline, potential optimizations
- Handling exact k blocks and base cases

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKBlocks(A, k){
  const n=A.length; const S=[0]; for(const x of A) S.push(S[S.length-1]+x);
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1e15));
  const prv=Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      dp[i][t]=dp[i-1][t];
      if(t>0){
        for(let l=1;l<=i;l++){
          const sum=S[i]-S[l-1];
          const val=dp[l-1][t-1]+sum*(i-l+1);
          if(val>dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; prv[i][t]=l; }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best:dp[n][k]};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to optimize to O(nk) with divide-and-conquer?; which pruning works?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests palindrome enumeration and multi-substring DP; requires handling non-overlapping constraints and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Palindrome precomputation isPal[l][r] in O(n^2).
- DP states dp[i][t] track best total length for first i chars and t palindromes.
- Transition closes a palindrome ending at i via isPal[l][i-1], adding (i-l+1).
- Backpointers enable reconstruction of the endpoints.
- Time: O(n^2 k); Space: O(n^2 + nk).

## Code Example
```javascript
// Precompute isPal
for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
  for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
    isPal[i][j] = s[i] === s[j] && (j - i < 2 || isPal[i + 1][j - 1]);
  }
}

// DP and reconstruction
const NEG = -Infinity;
let dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(NEG));
let prev = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));
dp[0][0] = 0;
for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
  for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
    dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
    if (t > 0) {
      for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
        if (isPal[l - 1][i - 1] && dp[l - 1][t - 1] !== NEG) {
          const cand = dp[l - 1][t - 1] + (i - l + 1);
          if (cand > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = cand;
            prev[i][t] = [l - 1, t - 1, i - 1];
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to structure DP with prefix states, reconstruct decisions, and enforce a gap between segments — a common real-world pattern in budgeting or scheduling.

## Key Concepts
- Kadane variant to track best subarray ending at i
- 2D DP dp[i][t] for first i elements and t blocks
- Reconstructing endpoints with backpointers; gap handling

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const endSum = Array(n).fill(0);
  const endStart = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (i === 0 || endSum[i-1] + A[i] < A[i]) {
      endSum[i] = A[i];
      endStart[i] = i;
    } else {
      endSum[i] = endSum[i-1] + A[i];
      endStart[i] = endStart[i-1];
    }
  }
  // dp and backtracking would follow (omitted for brevity)
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you relax the gap constraint or support at-most-k blocks?
- How does the solution change with negative numbers or ties in endpoint choices?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests DP skills with an additional dimension for tracking turns while handling obstacles and grid boundaries. It verifies understanding of initialization logic, edge cases, and how to aggregate the final answer by summing across valid turn counts.

## Key Concepts
- 4D DP over cells, turn count, and last direction
- Turn counting via direction changes during transitions
- Correct handling of obstacles and grid boundaries
- Time/space trade-offs and proper base case logic

## Code Example
```javascript
function countPaths(grid, K) {
  const m = grid.length;
  const n = grid[0].length;
  if (grid[0][0] === 1 || grid[m-1][n-1] === 1) return 0;

  const dp = Array.from({ length: m }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: n }, () =>
      Array.from({ length: K + 1 }, () => [0, 0])
    )
  );

  dp[0][0][0][0] = 1; // right
  dp[0][0][0][1] = 1; // down

  for (let i = 0; i < m; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {
      if (grid[i][j] === 1) continue;
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        // from left (coming from right)
        if (j > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][0] += dp[i][j-1][t][0]; // same direction
          if (t > 0) dp[i][j][t][0] += dp[i][j-1][t-1][1]; // turn
        }
        // from top (coming from down)
        if (i > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][1] += dp[i-1][j][t][1]; // same direction
          if (t > 0) dp[i][j][t][1] += dp[i-1][j][t-1][0]; // turn
        }
      }
    }
  }

  let ans = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
    ans += dp[m-1][n-1][t][0] + dp[m-1][n-1][t][1];
  }
  return ans;
}
```

This runs in O(m × n × K) time and O(m × n × K) space.

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests DP with non-overlapping selections and per-subarray costs, mirroring real scenarios like batch processing, budget allocation, or ad-slot optimization where exactly k intervals are chosen from a sequence.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping k-interval DP
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sum queries
- Maintaining a running best to optimize transitions from O(n) to O(1)
- Reconstruction of exact intervals

## DP Recurrence
Let pref[i] = sum of first i elements (pref[0] = 0).

**Transition:**
dp[i][t] = max(
  dp[i-1][t],                                    // Don't end a subarray at i
  best[t] + pref[i]                              // End a subarray at i
)

where best[t] = max_{1 ≤ l ≤ i} (dp[l-1][t-1] - pref[l-1])

**Initialization:**
dp[0][0] = 0, dp[0][t>0] = -∞

**Answer:** dp[n][k]

## Reconstruction
Track which choice was made at each dp[i][t]. If dp[i][t] came from dp[i-1][t], no subarray ends at i. Otherwise, find l where dp[l-1][t-1] - pref[l-1] = best[t], and record subarray (l..i).

## Complexity
- Time: O(nk) with O(1) transitions using running best
- Space: O(nk) for dp table, O(nk) for reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = [0];
  for (let x of A) pref.push(pref[pref.length - 1] + x);

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const choice = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      // Skip position i
      if (dp[i - 1][t] >= dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
        choice[i][t] = 0; // 0 = skip
      }

      // Start/extend subarray ending at i
      if (t > 0) {
        let best = -Infinity;
        for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
          const val = dp[l - 1][t - 1] + (pref[i] - pref[l - 1]) - c;
          if (val > best) best = val;
        }
        if (best > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = best;
          choice[i][t] = 1; // 1 = take subarray ending at i
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruction
  const subarrays = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    if (choice[i][t] === 1) {
      let l = 1;
      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const val = dp[j - 1][t - 1] + (pref[i] - pref[j - 1]) - c;
        if (val === dp[i][t]) {
          l = j;
          break;
        }
      }
      subarrays.unshift([l - 1, i - 1]); // Convert to 0-indexed
      t--;
    }
    i--;
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], subarrays };
